"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings include a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological stage pT3a with extraprostatic extension, but negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion are favorable. The absence of lymphovascular invasion is also a positive finding. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 6.2 and the lack of lymph node removal, as nodal status is documented as 'no lymph nodes removed,' which widens uncertainty as pNx is not equivalent to pN0. The biopsy finding, showing Gleason 3+4, aligns with the resection, but the biopsy was only one session.\n\nInformation that would revise this prognosis significantly would be the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data prevents a definitive assessment of recurrence risk. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence, but the uncertainty interval is wide. The lack of nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of local control and potential systemic spread. The fixed estimated horizon is 42.13 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 27.38–56.87 months; its coverage has not been validated."
